 "title": "Scramble Therapy for Better Conversations",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator discusses a technique called Scramble Therapy from the book How to Talk to Anyone by Leil Lowndes. The main lesson is that experiencing things outside your comfort zone gives you topics to connect with different people.",
 "key_points": [
  "Go out and do things you know absolutely nothing about every once in a while.",
  "Examples include attending a random workshop, trying a new sport, going to an unusual event, or learning something outside your interest.",
  "Doing this picks up a little bit of knowledge about parts of the world you did not know existed.",
  "When you meet someone interested in that thing, you actually have something to talk about.",
  "You do not need to be an expert; knowing enough to ask an interesting question makes a difference.",
  "To talk to many different kinds of people, you have to experience many different kinds of things."
 ],
 "content": "one of the most interesting things i’ve learnt about becoming better at conversations is that sometimes, it has very little to do with talking. i’ve been reading How to Talk to Anyone by Leil Lowndes, and this technique called Scramble Therapy really stood out to me. the idea is simple. go out and experience things you know absolutely nothing about. try a new sport, attend a random workshop, explore an unfamiliar interest, or just put yourself in environments you wouldn’t normally be in. you don’t need to become an expert at everything. even knowing a little about different things gives you more ways to connect with different people. and honestly, i love this idea because it’s something i’ve been trying to do in my own life too. the more of the world you experience, the more of the world you can relate to. still learning new things from this book, and i’ll keep sharing the interesting ones as i go :) follow @msurdeep for more on psychology, communication and personal growth. [how to talk to anyone, leil lowndes, scramble therapy, social skills, communication, psychology, curiosity, personal growth] Transcript: One of the easiest ways to become someone who can almost talk to anyone at any time. And surprisingly, it has very little to do with talking in itself. I've been reading this book called How to talk to anyone, and the author talks about this one therapy called scramble therapy. The idea is pretty simple. Once in a while, go out. Do something you know absolutely nothing about. For example, attending a random workshop, going and trying a new sport, going to an event you normally wouldn't go to, or learning something completely outside your interest. Because every time you do something new, you pick a little bit knowledge about a world that you didn't know existed before. And the next time you meet someone who's interested in that thing, you actually have something to talk about. You don't have to be an expert. Even knowing enough to ask an interesting question will make that difference. And I think that's what I love about this technique. Because if you want to be able to talk to a lot of different kinds of people, you have to start experiencing all kinds of things. And that's exactly what this technique allows. Text on screen: One technique to become someone who can talk to anyone (Using How to talk to anyone by Leil Lowndes)",
